## ⚙️ 5. Prerequisites

Before starting, make sure you have:

- Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 (or another supported Linux distribution).
- An AWS EC2 instance if you want to run the lab in AWS.
- A GitHub account and a new repository.
- An internet connection to download packages and Trivy vulnerability databases.
- Basic knowledge of Linux commands and Docker.

### Recommended EC2 setup

A small general-purpose EC2 instance is sufficient for this lightweight sample application and scans. Image scanning can use additional memory and disk depending on the image and vulnerability database.

**Security Group:** No inbound application port is required for this command-line lab. SSH (TCP 22) should be restricted to your trusted IP. Do not open port 8080 unless you separately choose to run and access the sample web application.

## 📥 6. Install Required Tools on Ubuntu EC2

### Step 1: Update the system and install utilities

```bash
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y curl wget git ca-certificates gnupg lsb-release
```

### Step 2: Install Docker

```bash
sudo apt-get install -y docker.io

# Start Docker now and enable it after reboot
sudo systemctl enable --now docker

# Allow the current user to run Docker commands
sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
```

**Important:** Log out and reconnect to EC2 so the new Docker group membership is applied. Then verify:

```bash
docker --version
docker ps
```

If you do not want to reconnect immediately, use `sudo docker` for Docker commands in the current session.

### Step 3: Install Trivy

Add the official Aqua Security Trivy APT repository:

```bash
sudo apt-get install -y wget apt-transport-https gnupg

wget -qO - https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/deb/public.key \
  | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/trivy.gpg

echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/trivy.gpg] https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/deb generic main" \
  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/trivy.list

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y trivy
```

### Step 4: Verify installations

```bash
docker --version
trivy --version
git --version
```

Official installation guide: https://trivy.dev/latest/getting-started/installation/

